# Copy to infra/.env. Local development values only.
POSTGRES_USER=cellar
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=cellar
POSTGRES_DB=cellar
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

MINIO_ROOT_USER=cellar
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=cellar-dev-secret
MINIO_BUCKET=cellar-files
# The PUBLISHED host port, and therefore the port inside every presigned URL a
# BROWSER is given: the `actors` service's FILES_S3_PORT follows it, and so does
# FILES_S3_PUBLIC_URL below. Moving it means rebuilding the client image,
# because that origin is baked into the build's CSP.
MINIO_PORT=9100
MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9101

# --- E3 · MinIO has two addresses, and which one a URL carries matters -------
# SigV4 signs the `Host` header, so whoever dials a presigned URL must dial the
# exact authority it was signed for — it cannot be proxied, aliased or
# rewritten. And on Docker Desktop for macOS no single address means MinIO from
# both a host browser and a container (measured, with every candidate, in the
# E3 block of infra/docker-compose.yml). So there are two:
#
#   browser-facing   localhost:9100   FILES_S3_ENDPOINT / FILES_S3_PORT
#   in-network       minio:9000       FILES_S3_INTERNAL_ENDPOINT / …_PORT
#
# Both are pinned as literals in infra/docker-compose.yml rather than read from
# here, because the browser-facing pair must agree with the client image's build
# arg below and a variable that moved only one of them would surface as a
# blocked upload rather than a startup error. Production sets only the first
# pair, to a real hostname that resolves internally too, and the split
# disappears (services/actors/src/lib/s3-presign.ts explains why that is safe).
#
# The one knob worth having here is the client build arg, which must equal
# `http://${FILES_S3_ENDPOINT}:${FILES_S3_PORT}`:
FILES_S3_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:9100

API_PORT=3001
ACTORS_PORT=3002
# Grafana (otel-lgtm). Anonymous ADMIN on this port in the local lanes, by
# design — it is only safe because BIND_ADDR (bottom of this file) defaults to
# loopback. Its data persists in the project's otel-lgtm-data volume until
# `docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml down -v` / `dev:down -- --volumes`.
GRAFANA_PORT=3010
# Host port for the CONTAINERISED Next client (`client` in
# infra/docker-compose.yml). Not 3000: that stays free for the host-run
# `bun run dev`, so both can be up at once. Deliberately not named WEB_PORT —
# `bun run dev:env` exports that for the host lane's own derived port.
CLIENT_PORT=3003

# --- Alert delivery · Grafana -> Discord -------------------------------------
# WHERE AN ALERT ACTUALLY ENDS UP. Until this variable existed, every rule in
# infra/grafana/provisioning/alerting/ routed to Grafana's built-in
# `grafana-default-email` receiver, which has no SMTP behind it — so the alerts
# reached whoever happened to open the Alerting page, i.e. nobody. The
# receiver that replaces it is provisioned in
# infra/grafana/provisioning/alerting/contact-points-and-policies.yaml, which
# reads this variable out of the `otel-lgtm` container's environment as
# `$DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL`.
#
# NO VALUE HERE, EVER. This repository is public, and a Discord webhook URL is
# a credential: anyone holding it can post to that channel as the integration,
# with no further authentication. The real value belongs in the gitignored
# infra/.env beside this file. The same secret already exists as the GitHub
# repository secret DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL (used by
# .github/workflows/release-discord.yaml); GitHub stores secrets write-only, so
# it cannot be read back out — copy it from Discord (Server Settings ->
# Integrations -> Webhooks -> Copy Webhook URL) or from wherever you kept it.
#
# ⚠ AN UNSET VALUE FAILS SILENTLY. Measured against grafana/otel-lgtm:latest
# (Grafana 13.2.0, 2026-09-18): Grafana expands a missing variable to the empty
# string, logs no warning, and provisions the contact point anyway. Grafana
# starting cleanly is therefore NOT evidence that alerting can deliver. The
# only proof is a notification arriving; the contact-points file has the
# one-line curl that sends a real test message.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Both lanes now wire this through. `infra/docker-compose.yml`'s `otel-lgtm`
# service mounts ./grafana/provisioning and passes DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL (094bf384);
# the prod file is an overlay on it, so it inherits both, and
# infra/.env.prod.example lists the variable (1c403299).
#
# This block used to say the dev lane had neither — measured 2026-09-18, when
# `cellar-stack-otel-lgtm-1` reported `Mounts: []` and the provisioning API
# returned `[]` — and suggested `DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL: ${DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL:-}`.
# Do NOT use that form: an EMPTY Discord url is a fatal provisioning error on
# Grafana 13.2.0 and stops Grafana booting. The compose file defaults it to
# `https://discord.invalid/alerts-not-configured` instead, so an unset value
# boots cleanly and fails only at delivery, visibly, in Grafana's log.
#
# That default is also the trap: leave this unset and alerts go nowhere while
# the Alerting page looks healthy. Set a real webhook in infra/.env.

# --- A7 · services/api ------------------------------------------------------
# Where the API fetches better-auth's JWKS. Service-internal on compose; set it
# to http://localhost:3002/api/auth/jwks when running the API outside compose.
AUTH_JWKS_URL=http://actors:3002/api/auth/jwks

# --- A6 · better-auth (services/actors) -------------------------------------
# better-auth's five tables live in POSTGRES_DB alongside the domain tables
# since X2, so there is no second database and no AUTH_POSTGRES_DB. The actors
# service derives AUTH_DATABASE_URL from POSTGRES_* (infra/docker-compose.yml).
# Session/JWKS encryption secret. **Deliberately blank — fill it in, per
# worktree, with `openssl rand -base64 32`** (or let `bun run dev:doctor --fix`
# do it, which is the same command).
#
# E5b: this line used to ship a 44-character constant. It was obviously
# placeholder-shaped, which is exactly why nobody looked at it twice — and
# nothing refused it, so it became the live signing key of every local stack
# that copied this file, including the committed `cellar-stack`. better-auth
# encrypts the JWKS *private* key at rest keyed off this value (`jwks`'
# `disablePrivateKeyEncryption` is left at its default in
# `services/actors/src/auth/auth.ts`), so anyone holding the repository could
# decrypt the stored private key and mint a `role:"admin"` token for any `sub`
# against any instance still running it. Blank fails closed instead:
# `readAuthConfig` throws `[auth] BETTER_AUTH_SECRET is required`, and it also
# now refuses the old constant by name — see `auth/config.ts`.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# Public origin of the actors app; better-auth builds callback URLs from it.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3002
# Comma-separated origins allowed to start an auth flow (the Next app). This is
# the HOST dev server's origin; the containerised client's
# (http://localhost:${CLIENT_PORT:-3003}) is appended by
# infra/docker-compose.yml, so it does not need repeating here — and an
# override of this variable cannot accidentally un-trust it.
AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
# Transparent bcrypt -> scrypt upgrade after a successful sign-in. "false" disables.
AUTH_REHASH_ON_SIGNIN=true

# OAuth. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY — real values belong in the gitignored infra/.env.
# Register these redirect URIs with each provider (BETTER_AUTH_URL + the path):
#   Google   http://localhost:3002/api/auth/callback/google
#   Facebook http://localhost:3002/api/auth/callback/facebook
#   Discord  http://localhost:3002/api/auth/callback/discord
# A provider is only enabled when BOTH halves are set.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
FACEBOOK_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
FACEBOOK_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
DISCORD_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
DISCORD_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Dapr API tokens (both sidecars, both apps) ------------------------------
# Leave both commented to use the published development defaults that
# infra/docker-compose.yml and `bun run dev:up` fall back to — a local stack
# then enforces tokens exactly as production does, with values everyone knows.
# Production requires its own (infra/.env.prod.example). What each one gates:
# docs/architecture/target-stack.md, "Dapr API tokens".
# DAPR_API_TOKEN=
# APP_API_TOKEN=

# --- X1 · AI provider (services/actors) --------------------------------------
# Which provider backs semantic search, menu scanning, recipe photos, item
# onboarding and tier-list insights. One of:
#   ollama, openai-compatible, google-ai, vertex-ai
#
# THE STANDING DECISION (docs/architecture/e4-decisions.md §12, 2026-09-17):
#   local dev on the Mac -> a local model via `openai-compatible` below,
#                           preferring llama-server or LM Studio (Metal);
#                           vLLM is supported but CPU-only on Apple silicon
#   deployed             -> the Gemini models on `vertex-ai`
#
# The value below stays `ollama` because it is the only provider that works with
# nothing started and no credentials, and it is what the acceptance suite runs
# against. Opting in to vLLM is two variables; see the block below.
#
# `ollama` is the local default and needs NO credentials. Two commands:
#   ollama serve                       (usually already running)
#   ollama pull nomic-embed-text       768-dim embeddings — the width of every
#                                      halfvec column in this database
#   ollama pull gemma3:4b              multimodal: text + the three vision seams
#
# Unset AI_PROVIDER entirely to run with no model. That is a supported state:
# every AI-backed feature then fails with an error naming itself, and nothing
# else is affected. What is NOT supported is a provider that is set but
# incomplete — the actor host refuses to start, on purpose. There is no stub
# and no fallback anywhere in services/actors/src/lib/ai/.
AI_PROVIDER=ollama

# Ollama runs on the host, not in the compose network.
#   from inside compose:  http://host.docker.internal:11434
#   running actors directly on the host: http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:11434
OLLAMA_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
OLLAMA_MODEL_LOW=gemma3:4b
OLLAMA_MODEL_MEDIUM=gemma3:4b
# gemma3:12b is the upstream default for the "high" tier and is a much larger
# pull; gemma3:4b keeps a laptop usable and is what the local stack ships with.
OLLAMA_MODEL_HIGH=gemma3:4b

# --- openai-compatible: the sanctioned local-dev provider --------------------
# Named for a WIRE FORMAT, not a product. Anything serving
# `POST /v1/chat/completions` and `POST /v1/embeddings` works here with no code
# change: vLLM (the sanctioned one), llama.cpp's llama-server, LM Studio, an
# MLX-backed shim, Ollama's own /v1 route, or api.openai.com.
#
# Getting a server up and PROVING it answers correctly:
#   scripts/ai/local-model.sh install     # the vLLM venv, outside the repo
#   scripts/ai/local-model.sh up          # both servers
#   scripts/ai/local-model.sh verify      # width, degeneracy, abstention
#   scripts/ai/local-model.sh env         # the exports, ready to paste
#
# PREFER llama-server OR LM STUDIO ON A MAC. vLLM has no Metal backend: it
# installs and runs on Apple silicon, CPU-only, and measured 52.4 s per image
# against 1.16 s on MPS (docs/architecture/findings/vllm-provider.md §6). It is
# actually FASTER than MPS on short text (95 ms vs 226 ms) — the problem is
# images, and four of the seven seams are vision seams. llama-server and LM
# Studio are Metal-accelerated and need no code change, just a different
# OPENAI_COMPAT_ENDPOINT and model name.
#
# IF YOU RUN vLLM ANYWAY, --no-enable-prefix-caching IS MANDATORY. It is on by
# default, and with it on 3 of 6 embeddings came back silently corrupted:
# unit-norm, no NaN, no error, one at cosine -0.0016 against the reference
# (§6.6). `scripts/ai/local-model.sh up` passes it; `verify` checks for it.
#
# SWITCHING PROVIDER IS A RE-EMBED, not a config flip. EmbeddingTaskType is a
# no-op on ollama and an instruction prefix here, so moving between them in
# either direction invalidates every stored vector. Both are 768 wide, so
# nothing errors — retrieval just quietly degrades. See
# services/actors/src/lib/embeddings.ts.
#
# vLLM serves ONE MODEL PER PROCESS, which is why there are two endpoints.
# Every other compatible server serves both routes from one base, and leaving
# the embedding endpoint unset defaults it to the chat one — so for LM Studio
# or llama-server, set only the first.
#   LM Studio:    http://host.docker.internal:1234
#   llama-server: http://host.docker.internal:8080
#   vLLM:         :8000 chat and :8001 embeddings, as below
#OPENAI_COMPAT_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:8000
#OPENAI_COMPAT_EMBEDDING_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:8001
#OPENAI_COMPAT_MODEL_LOW=Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct
#OPENAI_COMPAT_MODEL_MEDIUM=Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct
#OPENAI_COMPAT_MODEL_HIGH=Qwen/Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct
#OPENAI_COMPAT_EMBEDDING_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B
# Optional. Absent means no Authorization header, which is what a local server
# wants. Set it if you started vLLM with --api-key, or are pointing at OpenAI.
#OPENAI_COMPAT_API_KEY=
# Optional. Unset lets the server use the model's full context, which is the
# right default — guessing low truncates a menu extraction into invalid JSON.
#OPENAI_COMPAT_MAX_TOKENS=
# Truncate + RENORMALISE an over-wide embedding to AI_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS.
# Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B emits 2048 natively, so it needs either this or
# `--override-pooler-config '{"dimensions": 768}'` on the server (preferred).
# ONLY set this for a model documented as Matryoshka. Truncating one that is
# not yields a unit-norm vector that inserts happily and means nothing.
#OPENAI_COMPAT_EMBEDDING_TRUNCATE=false

# Applies to every provider that lets a caller ask for a width.
AI_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=768
AI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# --- AI spend overrides · BudgetActor (services/actors) -----------------------
# Both optional; unset or empty means "no overrides" (the parsers in
# services/actors/src/actors/budget-actor.ts return {} for a blank string).
# Read once per BudgetActor activation, so a change needs the actor host
# restarted. A malformed entry makes BudgetActor throw a ValidationError rather
# than silently metering at list price.
#
# Per-model prices, `model=<input>/<output>` in NANOCENTS PER 1000 TOKENS,
# comma-separated. Keys are longest-prefix matched against the model name.
#AI_MODEL_PRICES=gemini-3.5-flash-lite=30000000/250000000
#
# Per-seam monthly request caps, `seam=<count>`, comma-separated. Seams:
# embedding, item_defaults, menu_extraction, menu_match, place_review,
# recipe_photo, tier_list_insights.
#AI_BUDGET_MAX_REQUESTS=embedding=50000
#
# Per-USER caps, `service/endpoint=<hourly>/<daily>` over rolling windows,
# comma-separated, layered over the defaults in budget-actor.ts (USER_CAPS) —
# one account's share of a seam, where the two above bound the whole bill.
# Covers google_places/* as well as ai_model/*; a delivery's calls count
# against the user who caused it.
#BUDGET_USER_CAPS=ai_model/embedding=600/4000

# --- Hosted providers. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY --------------------------------
# Real values belong in the gitignored infra/.env. None of these is set in this
# repository, and none of the code below will invent one.
#
# google-ai — one key, from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY=
# The embedding model defaults to gemini-embedding-2 (config.ts
# GEMINI_EMBEDDING_MODEL, production's model). Overriding it changes the vector
# space: every stored vector must then be re-embedded (VectorReembedJobActor,
# `bun scripts/operator.ts reembed`). Forwarded by infra/docker-compose.yml
# and scripts/stack/stack.sh (empty there means the default), like every
# model override in this file.
#GOOGLE_AI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-2
# Chat models: leave these commented out to get the code defaults
# (config.ts GEMINI_CHAT_MODELS), so the next default change reaches you.
# These were pinned to gemini-2.5-*, which Vertex retires on 2026-10-20.
#GOOGLE_AI_MODEL_LOW=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
#GOOGLE_AI_MODEL_MEDIUM=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
#GOOGLE_AI_MODEL_HIGH=gemini-3.5-flash

# vertex-ai — THE DEPLOYED PROVIDER (e4-decisions.md §12). A project plus a
# service-account key with `roles/aiplatform.user`. The key is read from the
# environment only; there is no checked-in credentials file and no database
# lane for it. See docs/architecture/deploy-loki.md §2.7.
# Supply the key as a file path OR inline JSON; inline is what a secrets manager
# usually hands you. The Nhost lane that read the key out of the
# `admin_credentials` table is NOT ported (see services/actors/src/lib/ai/config.ts).
GOOGLE_GCP_PROJECT_ID=
# Gemini models are served from the `global` endpoint.
GOOGLE_GCP_LOCATION=global
# Leave EMPTY: the embedding model then picks its own endpoint —
# gemini-embedding-2 (the default) is served ONLY at `global` via :embedContent;
# text-embedding-* only regionally via :predict, so it goes to us-central1.
# A value here overrides both: a region 404s every gemini-embedding-2 call,
# `global` 404s every text-embedding-* one.
VERTEX_AI_EMBEDDING_LOCATION=
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON=
# Defaults to gemini-embedding-2; same re-embed caveat as
# GOOGLE_AI_EMBEDDING_MODEL above.
#VERTEX_AI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-2
# Same as the GOOGLE_AI_MODEL_* note above: unset means the code defaults.
#VERTEX_AI_MODEL_LOW=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
#VERTEX_AI_MODEL_MEDIUM=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
#VERTEX_AI_MODEL_HIGH=gemini-3.5-flash

# Host interface the dev stack publishes on. Defaults to loopback.
#
# This is a security default, not a preference. The actor host on ACTORS_PORT
# has NO authentication of its own -- it trusts the `ctx` in the request body,
# so any caller that can reach it may pass {"kind":"system"} and read or write
# the whole database. Production closes these ports entirely (`ports: !reset []`
# in docker-compose.prod.yml, with the edge exposing only /api/auth/*); locally the
# only thing standing in the way is which interface Docker binds.
#
# Set 0.0.0.0 ONLY on a network you trust, and only while you need it -- e.g.
# to open the PWA on a phone against your dev machine. It exposes every service
# below, including Postgres, to your whole LAN.
BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
